{"content_hash": "07c93d6a2f0f72b5", "version_id": 202, "article_id": 21, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Spanish Woman, 87, Dies After Eviction; Protests Continue", "title_zh": "西班牙87岁老妇被赶出家门后去世，抗议还在继续", "topic": "world", "source": "bbc_world", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6e3x21ev9wwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/d9ee/live/06010330-c283-11f1-98ea-35e6bf307fc9.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 230, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "An 87-year-old Spanish woman lost her home because she could not pay the rent. Her eviction made many people angry. Now she has died, and protests continue in Madrid.", "lead_zh": "西班牙一位87岁的老奶奶因为付不起房租，被赶出了家门。这件事让很多人非常生气。现在她去世了，马德里的抗议还在继续。", "preview": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her apartment in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out on a ", "preview_zh": "玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她在马德里的公寓里住了70多年。9月23日，警察用担架把她抬了出来。她付不起更高的房租。后来公司说，她可以回来，少付一点钱。但她当时在医院，不能签协议。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 192, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/audio/21/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 98.062, "size_bytes": 592128, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 278, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/data/versions/202.json", "paragraphs": ["Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her apartment in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out on a stretcher. She could not pay the higher rent. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the agreement.", "On 7 October, the Madrid Tenants' Union said she died. They did not say why. In Puerta del Sol, a big square in Madrid, people were very sad. They lit candles and stood quietly for one minute. Some wrote on signs: \"It's not a death, it's a killing\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\".", "Hundreds of people walked to the Spanish Congress building. They shouted, \"We will not accept one more eviction\" and \"The fight will continue\". Police put up barriers. Some protesters think the police help the evictions. The camping in the square is now 12 nights old. Protesters say it will continue \"as long as necessary\".", "Abascal's father rented the flat in 1956. The rent was capped for many years. A company bought the building in 2018 and raised the rent from €500 to €2,650 a month. Later they offered €1,650, but her pension was €1,350. She became a symbol of Spain's housing crisis. The government tried to pass emergency housing laws, but Congress rejected them last week."], "paragraphs_zh": ["玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她在马德里的公寓里住了70多年。9月23日，警察用担架把她抬了出来。她付不起更高的房租。后来公司说，她可以回来，少付一点钱。但她当时在医院，不能签协议。", "10月7日，马德里租户联盟说，她去世了。他们没有说原因。在马德里的大广场太阳门，人们非常难过。他们点起蜡烛，安静地站了一分钟。有些人在牌子上写：“这不是死亡，这是杀害”和“玛丽·卡门，全国的老奶奶”。", "几百人走到西班牙国会大楼。他们喊：“我们不会再接受一次驱逐”和“斗争会继续”。警察设置了路障。一些抗议者认为警察帮助驱逐。广场上的露营已经持续了12个晚上。抗议者说，会继续“只要有必要”。", "阿巴斯卡尔的父亲在1956年租了这套房子。很多年里，房租有上限。一家公司在2018年买下这栋楼，把房租从每月500欧元涨到2650欧元。后来他们提出1650欧元，但她的养老金只有1350欧元。她成了西班牙住房危机的象征。政府试图通过紧急住房法，但上周被国会否决了。"], "body": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her apartment in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out on a stretcher. She could not pay the higher rent. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the agreement.\n\nOn 7 October, the Madrid Tenants' Union said she died. They did not say why. In Puerta del Sol, a big square in Madrid, people were very sad. They lit candles and stood quietly for one minute. Some wrote on signs: \"It's not a death, it's a killing\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\".\n\nHundreds of people walked to the Spanish Congress building. They shouted, \"We will not accept one more eviction\" and \"The fight will continue\". Police put up barriers. Some protesters think the police help the evictions. The camping in the square is now 12 nights old. Protesters say it will continue \"as long as necessary\".\n\nAbascal's father rented the flat in 1956. The rent was capped for many years. A company bought the building in 2018 and raised the rent from €500 to €2,650 a month. Later they offered €1,650, but her pension was €1,350. She became a symbol of Spain's housing crisis. The government tried to pass emergency housing laws, but Congress rejected them last week.", "body_zh": "玛丽卡门·阿瓦斯卡尔87岁。她在马德里的公寓里住了70多年。9月23日，警察用担架把她抬了出来。她付不起更高的房租。后来公司说，她可以回来，少付一点钱。但她当时在医院，不能签协议。\n\n10月7日，马德里租户联盟说，她去世了。他们没有说原因。在马德里的大广场太阳门，人们非常难过。他们点起蜡烛，安静地站了一分钟。有些人在牌子上写：“这不是死亡，这是杀害”和“玛丽·卡门，全国的老奶奶”。\n\n几百人走到西班牙国会大楼。他们喊：“我们不会再接受一次驱逐”和“斗争会继续”。警察设置了路障。一些抗议者认为警察帮助驱逐。广场上的露营已经持续了12个晚上。抗议者说，会继续“只要有必要”。\n\n阿巴斯卡尔的父亲在1956年租了这套房子。很多年里，房租有上限。一家公司在2018年买下这栋楼，把房租从每月500欧元涨到2650欧元。后来他们提出1650欧元，但她的养老金只有1350欧元。她成了西班牙住房危机的象征。政府试图通过紧急住房法，但上周被国会否决了。", "text": "Maricarmen Abascal was 87 years old. She lived in her apartment in Madrid for more than 70 years. On 23 September, police took her out on a stretcher. She could not pay the higher rent. The company later said she could come back and pay less. But she was in hospital and could not sign the agreement. On 7 October, the Madrid Tenants' Union said she died. They did not say why. In Puerta del Sol, a big square in Madrid, people were very sad. They lit candles and stood quietly for one minute. Some wrote on signs: \"It's not a death, it's a killing\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\". Hundreds of people walked to the Spanish Congress building. They shouted, \"We will not accept one more eviction\" and \"The fight will continue\". Police put up barriers. Some protesters think the police help the evictions. The camping in the square is now 12 nights old. Protesters say it will continue \"as long as necessary\". Abascal's father rented the flat in 1956. The rent was capped for many years. A company bought the building in 2018 and raised the rent from €500 to €2,650 a month. Later they offered €1,650, but her pension was €1,350. She became a symbol of Spain's housing crisis. The government tried to pass emergency housing laws, but Congress rejected them last week.", "vocab": [{"word": "eviction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "驱逐；赶出家门", "note": "常指因付不起房租被赶出住所"}, {"word": "rent", "pos": "noun", "zh": "房租", "note": "也可以作动词，表示“租”"}, {"word": "protest", "pos": "noun/verb", "zh": "抗议", "note": "protest against something 抗议某事"}, {"word": "pension", "pos": "noun", "zh": "养老金", "note": "老年人每月领取的钱"}, {"word": "stretcher", "pos": "noun", "zh": "担架", "note": "医院用来抬病人的工具"}, {"word": "candle", "pos": "noun", "zh": "蜡烛", "note": "常用来表示悼念或祈福"}, {"word": "symbol", "pos": "noun", "zh": "象征", "note": "a symbol of ... ……的象征"}, {"word": "housing crisis", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "住房危机", "note": "指很多人租不起或买不起房的情况"}], "title_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says", "summary_original": "Maricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years in September.", "body_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says\nBy\nAlys Davies\nand\nAlba Morgade in Madrid\nPublished\n7 October 2026\nUpdated 59 minutes ago\nAn elderly woman whose eviction sparked nationwide protests over Spain's housing crisis and a political row that triggered early elections has died, according to Madrid's tenants union.\nMaricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years on 23 September after she was unable to afford rent increases.\nThe company that owned the flat later offered to allow her to return at a lower rate - but she was unable to sign an agreement as she had been admitted to hospital.\n\"We regret to announce that Maricarmen passed away today,\" the Madrid Tenants' Union said in a statement, adding her situation marked \"a turning point in the history of this country\".\nUnion spokesperson Alicia del Río said: \"Speculation costs lives, and today rentierism has finished killing our colleague Maricarmen\" but \"her memory lives on with us\".\nA cause of death was not announced.\nAbascal's death was received in Puerta del Sol with a big collective gasp, then followed by a minute of silence.\nIt is in this square in the centre of Madrid where thousands have been camping since Abascal's eviction last month to demand more affordable housing.\nOn Wednesday, it became the place to pay tribute to her.\nPeople lit dozens of candles around the square in a small improvised vigil that glowed with a warm light on a dry and warm autumn night.\nOther candles formed an altar to Abascal in front of the headquarters of the Community of Madrid government.\nMessages on cardboard banners next to the candles read \"it's not a death, it's a killing\", \"the fight will continue\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\", among others.\nA few hundred people then took to the streets that connect Puerta del Sol with the Spanish Congress, just 500m away, in an improvised protest.\nDuring the march people chanted: \"We will not tolerate a single eviction more\" and \"the fight will continue, Mari Carmen is still alive\". There were other chants against fascism and against the police.\nThe police had set up barriers to block the march. There was not a big police presence, and there was the tension that protests sometimes have.\nMany protesters seemed to feel the police have been complicit in the evictions, as under court orders they have to remove tenants who have been served eviction notices.\nAfter a couple of hours, the chants near Congress gave way to another minute of silence. Then, a big collective shout of \"murderers\".\nWednesday night's march has finished, but the camping, already on its 12th night, will continue \"as long as necessary\" one of the protesters told the BBC.\nAbascal became a symbol for Spain's housing crisis following her eviction.\nHer father first rented the property in 1956 and she continued as the tenant after her parents died, during which time there was a cap on the rent.\nHowever, Urbagestión, which bought the property in 2018, increased the rent from €500 per month (£428; $572) to €2,650 (£2,280; $3,020).\nThe company later offered to reduce this to €1,650 - still €300 more than Abascal's monthly pension.\nThree previous attempts to evict her were blocked by the courts and the case has received increasing media attention.\nAnger over the situation grew, leading to protests and putting pressure on the coalition government to approve emergency housing legislation -\nbut it was rejected in Congress last week\n.\nThat led to further protests,\nwith tens of thousands of people taking\nto the streets\nin cities across the country over the weekend.\nReferring to the rejected bills, the Madrid Tenants' Union said: \"While a historic agreement was reached that would have allowed her to return to her home, this solution has come too late. An absolutely avoidable situation after a torturing process.\"\nIt said the events surrounding her eviction represented a turning point in the country's history, but added that the cost to get to this point had been \"too high\".\n\"Above all, we will fulfil what she always asked of us: 'fight, keep fighting for what is yours',\" it said.\nIn a video recorded from her hospital bed following her eviction, Maricarmen said: \"I haven't managed to defend my home, but I have fought so that others can learn to fight for what's theirs.\"\n\"All my love goes to Maricarmen's family and loved ones,\" Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez said. \"A hug as well to everyone who today feels this loss as their own.\"\nOpposition People's Party leader Alberto Núñez Feijóo also sent his condolences to Maricarmen's loved ones, as he said on X: \"No campaign, no parties, no self-interested exploitation. No more stones in the wall.\n\"In the face of a person's death, only respect is in order,\" he added.\nEarlier on Wednesday, unions had called a general strike to take place on 11 November, demanding housing reforms.\nThe public outcry over Abascal's situation has led to political turmoil in Spain.\nOn Monday Sánchez, who leads a leftist coalition that does not hold a majority in parliament, called an early election for 29 November. It followed his failure to get the emergency housing legislation through Congress.\nThe political right had argued that the government's attempt to intervene in the housing market would have led to fewer homes being rented and would have raised prices further.\nSpeaking on Monday, Sánchez said he needed \"a broader progressive majority\" that can \"overcome the resistance of the elites\" to push through reforms.\nOther issues not relating to housing, such as numerous corruption scandals linked to his party, and pressures relating to the cost of living and immigration, have also caused him problems.\nMost opinion polls suggest the conservatives are likely to emerge as the largest party in the next election. However, they would be expected to need the support of the hard-right Vox party to form a government.\nAdditional reporting by Tom McArthur\nRelated topics\nSpain\nMore on this story\nSpanish PM Sánchez calls early election after housing protests\nPublished\n2 days ago\nDramatic eviction of woman aged 87 highlights Spain's housing shortage\nPublished\n23 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:16:22.071120", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Maricarmen", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.85, "sm": 100, "em": 850, "cs": 0, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Abascal", "s": 0.863, "e": 1.363, "sm": 862, "em": 1362, "cs": 11, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "was", "s": 1.375, "e": 1.613, "sm": 1375, "em": 1612, "cs": 19, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "87", "s": 1.625, "e": 1.875, "s